  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "regularization",
   "id": "b038",
   "spec": "Reduce the variance of the LightGBM base learner by averaging several models trained with different seeds. In base_predict, replace the single lgb.train call with a loop over 3 seeds (seed, seed+1, seed+2), each with its own lgb_params(...) and bagging_fraction=0.8 and bagging_freq=1 added to the params so the models differ. Average the predictions on Xb and return the mean as p_lgb. This keeps the same stacking interface and only changes how p_lgb is produced.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Seed-bagged LightGBM for variance reduction"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "data",
   "id": "b042",
   "spec": "In fit_predict, replace y with z = sign(y) * log1p(|y|) before the stack is trained (the oof loop, meta Ridge target and base_predict all see z). Invert the final prediction with sign(p) * expm1(|p|) before returning it. Leave the rest of the pipeline unchanged, so the descriptor, Tanimoto and RBF members all train on the transformed scale. The meta-learner is fit on transformed oof predictions, and the inversion happens only at the end.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Signed-log target transform"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "data",
   "id": "b046",
   "spec": "logD depends strongly on ionization, so add a small block of substructure counts to the feature matrix. Define about 10 SMARTS patterns: carboxylic acid [CX3](=O)[OX2H1,OX1-], tetrazole, acyl sulfonamide, phenol, aliphatic primary/secondary/tertiary amines (non-amide, non-aniline N with sp3 carbons), amidine/guanidine, pyridine-type aromatic N, and quaternary N. In featurize (or a helper called from fit_predict), compute per-molecule counts with Chem.MolFromSmiles(s).GetSubstructMatches for each pattern and hstack them as float32 columns after the descriptor block. Keep the Tanimoto kernel on the ECFP bits only, as now, and make sure the base_predict slicing for descriptors (Xa[:, 2*N_BITS:]) is updated so the new columns are included in the RBF kernel and the tree models.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Ionizable-group SMARTS count features"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 1,
   "family": "data",
   "id": "b050",
   "spec": "Append three columns to the feature matrix: RDKit Crippen MolLogP, TPSA and the count of hydrogen-bond donors and acceptors combined. Compute them from the same mol objects used for descriptors, cache per SMILES as desc_vector does, and clip to the 1st to 99th percentile range like the other descriptors. Keep these columns inside the LightGBM and RF inputs only, so the Tanimoto and RBF kernels still use the same columns as before. This is a small, cheap addition.",
   "status": "open",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Crippen logP and TPSA as a small physchem block"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "batch",
   "id": "b054",
   "spec": "In fit_predict, after computing the meta-learner and base_te once on the full training set, also run the whole base_predict on 4 extra bootstrap-by-scaffold-group resamples (sample groups with replacement, seed-driven). Reuse the same meta weights. Return the median of the 5 stack predictions. Cap the runtime by skipping the inner stacking OOF for the resamples, since the meta weights are reused.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Subagged stack with median aggregate"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "regularization",
   "id": "b058",
   "spec": "Both the random forest and the LightGBM member are stochastic, and a single draw adds variance on top of the genuine model error. Inside base_predict, fit each of them three times with random_state / seed values seed, seed + 1000 and seed + 2000, and average the three prediction vectors into a single column before returning. To stay inside the ten-minute limit, drop the random forest to 120 trees and LightGBM to 400 rounds so the total tree count is roughly unchanged. The kernel-ridge member is deterministic given its grid search and is left untouched, as is the stacking loop and the meta-learner.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Multi-seed averaging of the stochastic base learners"
  }
 ],
